  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n\nThe current approach uses local optimization at fixed grid sizes and function interpolation between grids. The key bottlenecks are:\n\n- O(n\u00b2) gradient computation for large grids\n- Limited exploration due to local optimization\n- Inefficient use of the available budget\n\nImprovements address these by:\n- Using a multi-phase strategy with increasing grid resolution\n- Adding radial basis function (RBF) projection for smooth interpolation\n- Implementing a global exploration phase followed by local search\n- Leveraging analytic gradient computation for faster optimization\n- Auto-tuning hyperparameters based on elapsed time\n\nThe new approach will start with the parent grid, optimize hyperparameters based on time usage, then:\n\nFirst: Find a near-optimal solution at the parent grid size (280 points) using low-temperature optimization to escape local minima\n\nSecond: Increase the grid resolution by doubling it (to 560 points) and rerunning optimization\n\nThird: Further double the resolution (to 1120 points) if time remains\n\nEach phase includes:\n- Budget allocation based on estimated optimization time\n- Early termination if time runs out\n- Coarse-grained random search followed by fine-grained local optimization\n- Temperature scheduling explicitly linking to grid size\n- Grid migration with radial basis interpolation\n\nThe expected improvement comes from:\n- More thorough grid exploration at optimal resolution\n- Improved interpolation technique reducing optimization time\n- Higher resolution allowing finer shaping of the function\n\nMax theoretical improvement from current 0.3810 to possibly 0.375-0.380 range\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n\nWe discreteize h as n-dimensional vector with:\n- Each component h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n- \u2211h[i] = n/2 (exactly)\n\nObjective: Minimize C_5 = max_k \u2211\u2096 h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nRepresented as:\nC_5 = (1/dx) * max_k \u2211\u2096 h(x) (1-h(x+k)) \n\nWith dx = 2/n\n\nConstraints are:\n- h[i] \u2208 [0,1] \u2200i\n- \u2211h[i] = n/2\n\nOptimization variables: h = (h\u2081, h\u2082, ..., h_n)\n\nGrid interpolation: For grid expansion, we use RBF interpolation:\n\u03c6(||x-y||) = exp(-\u03b3||x-y||\u00b2)\n\nGradient calculation:\nFor each spatial shift \u0394:\n\u2207h(x) = \u2211\u2096 (\u03b4_{x,i} * (1 - h(x+k) - h(x) * \u03b4_{x+k,i})\n\nThis gradient is computed efficiently using time-shifts\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n\nParent grid validation:\n- If parent_h provided, interpolate to new grid\n- Enforce integral=1 constraint via projection\n\nGrid migration procedures:\n```python\ndef interpolate_rbf(x_coarse, y_coarse, n_fine):\n    # Calculate coefficients for RBF interpolation\n    x_fine = np.linspace(0,2,n_fine)\n    gamma = 0.1  # chosen empirically\n    basis_matrix = np.zeros((n_fine, len(x_coarse)))\n    for i,xi in enumerate(x_coarse):\n        corr = np.abs(xi - x_fine.reshape(-1,1))\n        basis_matrix[:,i] = np.exp(-gamma*corr*corr)\n    \n    # Adjust for rank-deficiency\n    A = np.vstack([basis_matrix, np.ones(len(x_coarse))])\n    x_block = np.concatenate([y_coarse, np.ones(len(y_coarse))])\n    coeff, _, _, _ = np.linalg.lstsq(A, x_block, rcond=1e-10)\n    w = coeff[:-1]\n    return w[0][np.newaxis,:]  # First row is the actual interpolation\n```\n\nCore optimization using CVXOPT:\n```python\ndef optimize_with_cvxpy(h0, n_points, max_time=300, epsilon=1e-6):\n    n = n_points\n    dx = 2.0/n\n    \n    from cvxpy import Variable, Problem, Minimize, Constraint, Epigraph\n    from cvxpy.expressions import eps_parameter\n    \n    # Define variables\n    h = Variable(n)\n    \n    # Objective: max_k \u2211h(x)(1-h(x+k)) with max over shifts\n    h_vec = np.reshape(h, (n,), order='C')\n    x = np.linspace(0,2,n)\n    \n    # Prepare all cyclic shifts\n    H = np.zeros((n),dtype=float)\n    delays = np.arange(-n+1, n)\n    A = []\n    for lag in delays:\n        shift_h = np.roll(h_vec, lag)\n        if lag < 0:\n            shifted = np.concatenate([h_vec[-|lag|:], h_vec[:-|lag|]])\n            A.append(shifted + 1 - h_vec)\n        else:\n            A.append(h_vec + 1 - np.roll(h_vec, lag))\n    \n    A = np.array(A)\n    max_term = Epigraph(1.0 / dx * np.max( [np.dot(A[i], h.value) for i in range(len(A))] ), \n                        shape=(1,1))\n    \n    # Subject to:\n    c = np.zeros(n)\n    c[:n//2] = 1.0\n    c[n//2] = 0 if n%2==0 else 0.5  # Handle odd symmetry\n    c[n//2+1:] = -0.5 if n%2==0 else [-0.5]*(n//2)\n    \n    A_flat = np.concatenate([h.value, c])\n    b_flat = np.concatenate([np.array([1.0]), np.array([1.0])])\n\n    constraints = [h >= 0, h <= 1, h @ c == 1.0]\n\n    prob = Problem(Minimize(max_term), constraints)\n    \n    # Set options with time limit\n    options = {'verbose': False, 'max_iters': 1000, 'relative_gap': 1e-6}\n    result = prob.solve(solver=CVXOPT, **options)\n    \n    return h.value\n```\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n- Temperature schedule: auto-scales with grid size n\n- Grid expansion: 2x, 2x, 2x chain (280->560->1120)\n- Start strategies:\n  * Random feasible start at each grid\n  * Parent interpolation at grid expansion\n  * Multiple starts per grid point\n- Local-to-global bias:\n  * First stage: find wide-network solution\n  * Second stage: refine with local search\n  * Gradients are computed using finite differences for larger grids\n\nBudget and termination:\n- Time monitor at 5% intervals\n- Cooldown phase for grid migration\n- Solve without projection but with explicit constraints in formulation\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n\nDominant costs:\n- Objective evaluation: O(n\u00b2) likely due to matrix formation\n- Projection: O(n) per candidate\n- CVXOPT solves: O(n\u00b3) in worst-case but empirically faster\n\nFeasibility checks:\n- All h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n- Integral condition checked directly\n- Objective finite at all points\n\nFallback procedures:\n- Maintain best solution found so far\n- Time-expiry: return best solution with interpolation priority\n- Empty grid: use parent interpolation\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n\nFinal candidate validated by:\n1. Re-evaluate objective with original method\n2. Check finite value for both h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n3. Verify integral=1.0\n4. Ensure all lags were properly considered\n\nClean exit: Return tuple (h_values, best_c5, n_points)\n\nFinal code structure:\n```python\ndef run(seed=42, budget_s=1300, **kwargs):\n    start_time = time.time()\n    total_time = budget_s\n\n    # Phase 0: Initial assessment\n    remaining_time = total_time - (time.time() - start_time)\n    initial_n = 280\n    \n    # Phase 1: Coarse optimization on base grid\n    # (in_budget variant using adaptive timing)\n    \n    # Phase 2: Grid refinement and optimization\n    # Two more grid doublings if time remains\n    \n    # Phase 3: Final cleanup and return\n\n    # Return best solution found\n```\n\nFull implementation details:\n1. Maintain if statements for time monitoring\n2. Always consider projection fallbacks\n3. Budget-tuned solve iterations",
